I. Objective
You are an expert in PFAS adsorption studies. Your task is to extract the properties of all in-scope adsorbents from the source chunks. Each record is identified by Adsorbent_id (i.e., no two records should be assigned the same Adsorbent_id).

Your extraction is based on:
(1) Multiple text chunks from the same paper.
(2) Figure captions for figures that were mentioned in the provided chunks. If no figure was mentioned, this field will be empty.
(3) Adsorbent_list is a controlled vocabulary generated by an earlier study-level adsorbent enumeration process. It aims to list all adsorbents used in the current study and is organized into In-scope adsorbents and Out-of-scope adsorbents. Each entry may include Adsorbent_id, full name, abbreviation, commercial name, category, and subcategory when that information is extractable. Use In-scope adsorbents to standardize adsorbent identities only when the current source text supports the match. Use Out-of-scope adsorbents as an exclusion list: if a source adsorbent matches an out-of-scope entry, do not extract record for it. The provided Adsorbent_list is used for label standardization and scope disambiguation only; it is not evidence by itself.

II. Scope of Extraction
A candidate adsorbent record is in scope only if it satisfies all conditions below:

1. Target adsorbents are limited to the following categories for this project:
- Activated carbon, including AC, PAC, and GAC
- Ion-exchange resins
- Non-ionic resins
- Cyclodextrin polymers, including CDP, StyDex, and DEXSORB
- Modified clays/minerals

Adsorbents outside these categories are out of scope and must not be extracted, even if they appear in the source chunks. In most cases, the provided Adsorbent_list can be treated as the authoritative source for determining whether an adsorbent is in scope or out of scope. However, the list may occasionally contain errors or may omit some adsorbents mentioned in the text. If an adsorbent relevant to a reported performance value is missing from the Adsorbent_list, determine whether it is in scope based on the target adsorbent categories defined above.

2. Target adsorbents must be used in a PFAS batch adsorption experiment. A PFAS batch adsorption experiment is a closed-vessel test in which a PFAS-containing liquid is contacted with a freshly prepared adsorbent to measure PFAS uptake or removal by adsorption under defined conditions, without external energy input. Adsorbent properties are generally intrinsic to the material and should not depend on how the adsorbent is used. However, if researchers modify or prepare a material for specific applications, extract only the properties of the form that was actually used in the PFAS batch adsorption experiment.

3. Extract only the properties of adsorbents that are applied in the current study.

