IV. Field Explanations
- Adsorbent_id is the unique identifier for each distinct adsorbent material. Obtain the Adsorbent_id for the record you are about to output by matching source mentions against any relevant fields in the In-scope adsorbents section of Adsorbent_list, but output only the corresponding adsorbent_id. Match adsorbent-specific information using name fields when the statement refers to a specific material, such as “adsorbent A has property B.” Match category-level information using adsorbent_category when the statement applies to a broader class, such as “all activated carbons were ground to a specified particle size”. In that case, assign the property to all adsorbents in Adsorbent_list that belong to the matching category. If group membership is ambiguous, do not assign the property to specific records. Treat cosmetic variants in capitalization, spacing, punctuation, hyphenation, pluralization, trademark symbols, and minor OCR noise as matches when the adsorbent identity remains clear.

- The meaning and recommended output format for each property field are specified below. Overall, it is combinations of <descriptor>, <measurement>, <unit>, where <descriptor> specifies what the value refers to (e.g., chemical name, water matrix), and <measurement> and <unit> denote the reported numeric value and its unit. For fields whose unit is part of the field name, convert to that unit when the conversion is unambiguous. Different fields may require different combinations of these components, the grouping provided below are recommended and should be applied when the source text supports them, but they are not mandatory. If any component is not supported by evidence in the provided source chunks, omit that component rather than inferring it. Do not introduce any additional components or formatting (e.g., parenthetical notes, alternative encodings, or free-form sentences) beyond these three components. 
